```js
updated(changedProperties) {
  if (changedProperties.has('error')) {
    // existing behaviour: mirror the error attribute onto each child
    this.items.forEach((el) => {
      if (this.error) el.setAttribute('error', '');
      else el.removeAttribute('error');
    });
  }

  if (changedProperties.has('validity') || changedProperties.has('error')) {
    const isInvalid = this.validity && this.validity !== 'valid';
    const radiogroup = this.shadowRoot.querySelector('[role="radiogroup"]');
    radiogroup.setAttribute('aria-invalid', isInvalid ? 'true' : 'false');
  }
}

render() {
  const isInvalid = this.validity && this.validity !== 'valid';

  return html`
    <fieldset
      role="radiogroup"
      aria-invalid=${isInvalid ? 'true' : 'false'}
      aria-describedby=${this.error ? 'errorMessage' : nothing}
    >
      <legend><slot name="legend"></slot></legend>
      <slot></slot>
      ${this.error
        ? html`<p id="errorMessage" role="alert" aria-live="assertive">${this.error}</p>`
        : nothing}
    </fieldset>
  `;
}
```

Key additions:
- `aria-invalid` on the `radiogroup` element itself, kept in sync with `validity` (not just `error`, so `valueMissing` from a plain `required` violation is also announced).
- `aria-describedby="errorMessage"` on the `radiogroup`, wired to the `id` on the error `<p>`, so screen readers associate the error text with the group even outside the live-region announcement moment (e.g., when the user navigates back to it later).

Setting `aria-invalid` directly in `render()` means it's always correct on first paint; the `updated()` block keeps it correct if `validity`/`error` change without a full re-render path (defensive redundancy — drop the `updated()` half if `render()` alone proves sufficient in your case).
